PART 6 — CRITICAL ACTIVE-BOOK LOCK SYSTEM

This is one of the most important requirements.

Every student must be restricted to one currently selected active textbook.

The selected book determines the complete learning context of the application.

This must be an actual functional restriction, not merely a decorative lock icon.

6.1 Book selection

During onboarding or first learning setup, the student chooses:

- Academic year.
- Class.
- Curriculum or appropriate board.
- Language or medium.
- Subject.
- One specific textbook.

Only real, verified textbook entries may appear.

6.2 Active-book behavior

After selecting a textbook:

- Assign its unique book ID to the student.
- Save it to persistent account data.
- Restore it after reopening the application.
- Synchronize it across authenticated devices.
- Display the active textbook prominently.
- Restrict the entire learning experience to that textbook.

The active textbook must control:

- Lessons.
- Chapters.
- Topics.
- Question banks.
- Practice sessions.
- Revision items.
- Mock examinations.
- Learning recommendations.
- Bookmarks.
- Textbook search.
- AI tutor references.
- Book progress.
- Chapter analytics.
- Competitive academic context.

No learning screen may silently mix material from another textbook.

6.3 Change-book workflow

Include an explicit Change Book operation.

Changing books must:

1. Show the currently active book.
2. Explain that the student is changing their learning context.
3. Ask for confirmation.
4. Let the student choose another verified textbook.
5. Update the active-book ID.
6. Preserve the previous book's progress and history.
7. Load the new book's learning content.
8. Prevent old and new book content from being mixed accidentally.

Keep one active book at any moment.

Other textbook catalogues may be opened through the deliberate Change Book flow.

Do not display several independently active books.

6.4 Enforcement

Implement active-book enforcement in both the frontend and backend.

Do not rely on hidden navigation buttons as security.

Every learning-related query must check the active book context.

Add automated tests proving that content from an unselected book cannot accidentally appear in the student's learning experience.

